{
  "schema_version": 2,
  "name": "awk — text processor",
  "description": "A miniature programming language for text processing. You feed awk lines of text (from a file or stdin), it splits each line into fields, and runs a tiny program you write against every line. Great for one-off tabular data work — extract a column from a CSV, sum a column of numbers, reformat a log file, count word frequencies — without writing a real script.\n\nNew to awk? Click the 'Help & reference' tab for a crash course (anatomy, auto-variables, common one-liners). Source: https://www.gnu.org/software/gawk/manual/",
  "executable": "awk",
  "working_directory": null,
  "sections": [
    { "name": "Program", "layout": "tab" },
    { "name": "Input", "layout": "tab" },
    { "name": "Variables", "layout": "tab" },
    { "name": "Compatibility", "layout": "tab" },
    { "name": "Help & reference", "layout": "tab" }
  ],
  "argument_template": [
    ["-F", "{field_separator}"],
    ["-v", "{var_assignment}"],
    "{posix?--posix}",
    "{traditional?--traditional}",
    "{characters_as_bytes?-b}",
    ["-f", "{program_file}"],
    "{program}",
    "{input_files}"
  ],
  "params": [
    {
      "id": "program",
      "label": "Program",
      "description": "The awk program — pattern { action } pairs. Examples to copy:\n\n  Print 1st column of a CSV:        {print $1}\n  Sum column 3:                     {sum += $3} END {print sum}\n  Filter to lines containing 'foo': /foo/ {print}\n  Number each line:                 {print NR, $0}\n  Print blank-line-separated:       /^$/ {print}\n  Average a column:                 {s += $1} END {print s/NR}\n\nWrap multi-statement programs in braces. Don't surround the whole thing in quotes — ScripTree passes it as a single argv token already.\n\nLeave blank if you're loading the program from 'Program file' below instead.",
      "type": "string",
      "widget": "textarea",
      "required": false,
      "default": "",
      "section": "Program"
    },
    {
      "id": "program_file",
      "label": "Program file (-f)",
      "description": "Read the awk program from this file instead of typing it inline. Useful for longer programs or when you reuse the same program a lot. If you fill in BOTH this and 'Program' above, awk will treat the inline program as another input filename — almost never what you want. Pick ONE source.",
      "type": "path",
      "widget": "file_open",
      "required": false,
      "default": "",
      "file_filter": "awk programs (*.awk);;All files (*)",
      "section": "Program"
    },
    {
      "id": "input_files",
      "label": "Input files",
      "description": "Files to process. Space-separated for multiple. Leave blank to read from stdin.\n\nawk will run the program against each file in turn (with FILENAME tracking the source). NR keeps counting across files; FNR resets per file.\n\nExamples:\n  /etc/passwd\n  data/*.csv\n  log1.txt log2.txt log3.txt",
      "type": "string",
      "widget": "text",
      "required": false,
      "default": "",
      "section": "Program"
    },
    {
      "id": "field_separator",
      "label": "Field separator (-F)",
      "description": "Character (or regex) that splits each input line into fields ($1, $2, ...). Default is whitespace (any run of spaces/tabs).\n\nCommon picks:\n  ,            comma — for CSV\n  \\t           literal tab — for TSV\n  :            colon — for /etc/passwd-style files\n  |            pipe — for pipe-separated data\n  [[:space:]]+ default-ish — any run of whitespace\n  ;,           multi-char regex — split on either ';' or ','\n\nLeave blank to keep the default whitespace behaviour. The value you type goes through awk's regex engine, so backslashes have to be escaped in some cases.",
      "type": "string",
      "widget": "text",
      "required": false,
      "default": "",
      "section": "Input"
    },
    {
      "id": "var_assignment",
      "label": "Variable (-v)",
      "description": "Set a variable before BEGIN runs, in the form NAME=VALUE. Useful when you want a parameter you can change without editing the program.\n\nExamples:\n  threshold=100         use as: $1 > threshold {print}\n  fmt=\"%-20s %d\\n\"      use in printf statements\n  IGNORECASE=1          gawk-only: case-insensitive matching\n\nFor multiple variables, set them inside a BEGIN block in the program instead — saves the form fight.",
      "type": "string",
      "widget": "text",
      "required": false,
      "default": "",
      "section": "Variables"
    },
    {
      "id": "posix",
      "label": "Strict POSIX mode (--posix)",
      "description": "(gawk-only) Disable all gawk-specific extensions. The program runs against the strict POSIX awk spec — no gensub(), no length(array), no @namespace, no FIELDWIDTHS, etc. Tick this when you're writing a script you want to ship to non-GNU environments (BSD awk, mawk, BusyBox awk).\n\nSafe to leave off if you're just hacking on data locally.",
      "type": "bool",
      "widget": "checkbox",
      "required": false,
      "default": false,
      "section": "Compatibility"
    },
    {
      "id": "traditional",
      "label": "Traditional / UNIX awk (--traditional)",
      "description": "(gawk-only) Even more strict than --posix — emulates original UNIX awk (V7) behaviour. POSIX features added later (gsub, sub, etc.) are still allowed but gawk extensions are off. Rarely needed; --posix covers most compat use.",
      "type": "bool",
      "widget": "checkbox",
      "required": false,
      "default": false,
      "section": "Compatibility"
    },
    {
      "id": "characters_as_bytes",
      "label": "Treat bytes, not characters (-b / --characters-as-bytes)",
      "description": "(gawk-only) Treat all input as raw bytes (single-byte locale) instead of multi-byte / UTF-8 characters. Use when the input is binary or in a non-UTF-8 encoding and gawk is mis-counting characters. Otherwise leave off — gawk handles UTF-8 correctly by default.",
      "type": "bool",
      "widget": "checkbox",
      "required": false,
      "default": false,
      "section": "Compatibility"
    },
    {
      "id": "_help_anatomy",
      "label": "Anatomy of an awk program",
      "description": "Reference only — this field has no effect on what's run. Read it, copy snippets out of it, ignore it.",
      "type": "string",
      "widget": "textarea",
      "required": false,
      "default": "An awk program is a list of pattern { action } pairs. For each input line, awk runs every action whose pattern matches.\n\n  pattern1 { action1 }\n  pattern2 { action2 }\n\nEither half can be omitted:\n  - 'no pattern'  = match every line\n  - 'no action'   = print the matching line ({ print } is the default action)\n\nTwo special patterns:\n  BEGIN { ... }   runs ONCE before any input is read\n  END   { ... }   runs ONCE after all input is consumed\n\nA tiny full example:\n\n  BEGIN { sum = 0 }\n  /^[0-9]/ { sum += $1 }\n  END { print \"Total:\", sum }\n\nThis sums the first column of every line that starts with a digit, then prints the total at the end.",
      "section": "Help & reference"
    },
    {
      "id": "_help_variables",
      "label": "Auto-variables awk gives you for free",
      "description": "Reference only — this field has no effect on what's run.",
      "type": "string",
      "widget": "textarea",
      "required": false,
      "default": "Awk pre-splits each line for you and exposes these built-in variables:\n\n  $0          the whole line\n  $1, $2, ... the 1st, 2nd, ... field (separator: whitespace by default; -F changes it)\n  $NF         the LAST field on the line (NF is the number of fields)\n  NF          number of fields on this line\n  NR          record number — i.e. line number, 1-based, counting across all files\n  FNR         line number within the current file (resets per file)\n  FILENAME    name of current input file\n  FS          input field separator (default: ' ' which means 'any whitespace run')\n  OFS         output field separator (default: a single space). Used when you 'print a, b, c'.\n  ORS         output record separator (default: newline)\n  RS          input record separator (default: newline)\n\nYou can change FS, OFS, etc. inside BEGIN { } to take effect before any input is read.",
      "section": "Help & reference"
    },
    {
      "id": "_help_oneliners",
      "label": "Common one-liners (copy/paste into 'Program')",
      "description": "Reference only — this field has no effect on what's run. Copy any of these into the Program tab.",
      "type": "string",
      "widget": "textarea",
      "required": false,
      "default": "PRINTING:\n  {print}                       print every line (cat clone)\n  {print $1}                    print 1st whitespace-separated field\n  {print $NF}                   print the LAST field\n  {print NR, $0}                number every line\n  {print NR\": \"$0}              number with a colon-separator\n\nFILTERING (no action = default { print }):\n  /error/                       print lines containing 'error'\n  !/DEBUG/                      print lines NOT containing 'DEBUG'\n  NF > 0                        print only non-blank lines\n  NF >= 5                       print lines with at least 5 fields\n  NR == 5                       print only line 5\n  NR >= 10 && NR <= 20          print lines 10-20\n  $1 ~ /^[0-9]+$/               print lines whose 1st field is all digits\n\nAGGREGATING:\n  {sum += $1} END {print sum}              sum the first column\n  {s += $1} END {print s/NR}                average the first column\n  END {print NR}                            count input lines\n  {count[$1]++} END {for (k in count) print k, count[k]}   count distinct values\n\nREFORMATTING:\n  BEGIN {OFS=\"\\t\"} {$1=$1; print}           re-emit fields tab-separated\n  -F, '{print $1, $3}'                      extract cols 1 & 3 from a CSV\n  {gsub(/foo/, \"bar\"); print}               sed-like substitute\n  {printf \"%-20s %d\\n\", $1, $2}             column-aligned output\n\nSee 'man awk' or https://www.gnu.org/software/gawk/manual/ for the full reference.",
      "section": "Help & reference"
    },
    {
      "id": "_help_form",
      "label": "About this form",
      "description": "Reference only — this field has no effect on what's run.",
      "type": "string",
      "widget": "textarea",
      "required": false,
      "default": "How the form maps to awk's CLI:\n\n  Program tab\n    Program          → the inline program (the positional 'awk_program' argv arg).\n                       Don't wrap it in quotes — ScripTree passes it as a single argv\n                       token already.\n    Program file     → -f program.awk. Use INSTEAD of inline, not in addition to.\n    Input files      → positional file arguments AFTER the program. Space-separated.\n                       Leave blank to read from stdin.\n\n  Input tab\n    Field separator  → -F (default: whitespace). Common picks: ',' ':' '\\t' '|'.\n\n  Variables tab\n    Variable         → -v NAME=VALUE. For multiple variables, set them inside a\n                       BEGIN block in the program instead — saves the form fight.\n\n  Compatibility tab  (gawk-only flags)\n    --posix         strict POSIX, disables gawk extensions\n    --traditional   even stricter — emulates original UNIX awk\n    -b              treat input as raw bytes, not UTF-8 characters\n\nIf 'awk' on your system isn't gawk, the Compatibility tab's flags are ignored or\nrejected (mawk and BSD awk don't recognize them). Set them only if you're targeting\ngawk.",
      "section": "Help & reference"
    }
  ],
  "source": {
    "mode": "manual",
    "url": "https://www.gnu.org/software/gawk/manual/",
    "help_text_cached": "awk [-F fs] [-v var=value] [-f program-file] [--posix] [--traditional] ['program'] [file ...]\n\nA programming language for pattern-directed scanning and processing.\n\n  -F FS              Field separator (default whitespace)\n  -v VAR=VALUE       Set variable before BEGIN\n  -f PROGFILE        Read program from file\n  -b                 Treat input as bytes, not characters (gawk)\n  --posix            Strict POSIX (gawk)\n  --traditional      UNIX awk compat (gawk)\n\nProgram structure: pattern { action } [pattern { action }]...\n  - BEGIN/END are special patterns run once before/after input.\n  - Each input line is split into $1, $2, ... by FS.\n  - Default action is { print }.\n\nMan: man awk\nGNU awk manual: https://www.gnu.org/software/gawk/manual/"
  }
}
